---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="Documentation | Twig — JSON, YAML & Data Explorer">
    <div class="docs">
        <details class="docs-toc-mobile">
            <summary>
                <span>Contents</span>
                <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
                    <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9" />
                </svg>
            </summary>
            <ul>
                <li><a href="#introduction">Introduction</a></li>
                <li><a href="#installation">Installation</a></li>
                <li><a href="#quickstart">Quickstart</a></li>
                <li><a href="#keybindings">Keyboard shortcuts</a></li>
                <li><a href="#cli">CLI reference</a></li>
                <li><a href="#configuration">Configuration</a></li>
                <li><a href="#troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a></li>
            </ul>
        </details>

        <aside class="docs-toc">
            <p class="toc-label">Contents</p>
            <ul>
                <li><a href="#introduction">Introduction</a></li>
                <li><a href="#installation">Installation</a></li>
                <li><a href="#quickstart">Quickstart</a></li>
                <li><a href="#keybindings">Keyboard shortcuts</a></li>
                <li><a href="#cli">CLI reference</a></li>
                <li><a href="#configuration">Configuration</a></li>
                <li><a href="#troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a></li>
            </ul>
        </aside>

        <article class="docs-content">
            <header class="docs-header">
                <p class="eyebrow">Documentation</p>
                <h1>Twig, end to end.</h1>
                <p class="lead">
                    A single-binary terminal UI for exploring
                    <strong>JSON</strong> and <strong>YAML</strong>. This guide
                    covers installation, the keymap, every CLI flag, and the
                    things that go wrong.
                </p>
            </header>

            <section id="introduction">
                <h2>Introduction</h2>
                <p>
                    Twig is a high-performance, terminal-based data explorer
                    for <strong>JSON</strong> and <strong>YAML</strong>. It
                    bridges the gap between simple text viewers (like
                    <code>less</code>) and full-blown IDEs.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Designed for developers who work with large datasets,
                    Kubernetes manifests, log exports, and complex
                    configuration files, Twig parses data into a navigable
                    tree structure and lets you search, jump, and inspect
                    without ever leaving the terminal.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Since v3.0.0, Twig is a single ~4 MB static binary
                    written in Rust. Drop it on any Linux, macOS, or Windows
                    machine and it runs — no Python, no runtime.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section id="installation">
                <h2>Installation</h2>
                <p>
                    The recommended way to install Twig is the one-line
                    installer — it downloads the matching prebuilt binary
                    from the latest GitHub release, verifies its SHA-256
                    checksum against the published
                    <code>*.sha256</code> file, and installs it to
                    <code>~/.local/bin</code> (or <code>/usr/local/bin</code>
                    when writable).
                </p>

                <h3>One-line installer</h3>
                <pre><code>curl -fsSL https://twig.wtf/install.sh | sh</code></pre>

                <h3>Useful flags</h3>
                <pre><code># Pin a specific release
curl -fsSL https://twig.wtf/install.sh | sh -s -- --version v3.0.0

# Install to a custom directory
curl -fsSL https://twig.wtf/install.sh | sh -s -- --to /usr/local/bin

# Build from source instead of downloading
curl -fsSL https://twig.wtf/install.sh | sh -s -- --method build

# Skip the install confirmation prompt
curl -fsSL https://twig.wtf/install.sh | sh -s -- --yes</code></pre>
                <p>
                    Run <code>curl -fsSL https://twig.wtf/install.sh | sh -s
                    -- --help</code> for the full flag list.
                </p>

                <h3>Cargo (from the GitHub repo)</h3>
                <p>
                    The <code>twig</code> crate name on <a
                        href="https://crates.io/crates/twig"
                        rel="noopener">crates.io</a
                    > belongs to an unrelated Rust templating engine, so we
                    don't publish our binary there. To build from source with
                    Cargo, install directly from the GitHub repo:
                </p>
                <pre><code>cargo install --git https://github.com/workdone0/twig twig --locked</code></pre>

                <h3>Manual download</h3>
                <p>
                    Prebuilt <code>.tar.gz</code> archives with matching
                    <code>*.sha256</code> checksum files are on the
                    <a
                        href="https://github.com/workdone0/twig/releases/latest"
                        rel="noopener">Releases page</a
                    >.
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Linux: <code>x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu</code>, <code
                            >aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu</code
                        ></li>
                    <li>
                        macOS: <code>x86_64-apple-darwin</code> (Intel),
                        <code>aarch64-apple-darwin</code> (Apple Silicon)
                    </li>
                    <li>Windows: <code>x86_64-pc-windows-msvc</code></li>
                </ul>

                <h3>Build from source</h3>
                <p>Requires Rust 1.75 or later.</p>
                <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/workdone0/twig.git
cd twig
cargo build --release
./target/release/twig --help</code></pre>

                <h3>Legacy Python (≤ v2.1.4)</h3>
                <p>
                    Older releases shipped to PyPI as <code>twg</code>. That
                    implementation is preserved on the
                    <a
                        href="https://github.com/workdone0/twig/tree/legacy-python"
                        rel="noopener"><code>legacy-python</code></a
                    > branch for reference. New development happens on
                    <code>master</code>. To migrate:
                </p>
                <pre><code>uv tool uninstall twg                       # remove old
curl -fsSL https://twig.wtf/install.sh | sh # install new</code></pre>
            </section>

            <section id="quickstart">
                <h2>Quickstart</h2>

                <h3>Open a file</h3>
                <p>
                    Pass a JSON or YAML file as the argument. Format is
                    auto-detected from the extension.
                </p>
                <pre><code>twig data.json     # or twig config.yaml</code></pre>

                <h3>Repair malformed JSON</h3>
                <p>
                    <code>--fix</code> attempts to repair common
                    malformations — trailing commas, unquoted keys,
                    single quotes, <code>NaN</code>/<code>Infinity</code> —
                    and writes a clean, round-trippable copy.
                </p>
                <pre><code>twig --fix bad.json -o clean.json</code></pre>

                <h3>Pretty-print to stdout</h3>
                <p>
                    Non-TUI mode that emits a colored, indented copy of
                    the file.
                </p>
                <pre><code>twig -p large.json &gt; pretty.json</code></pre>

                <h3>Benchmark streaming ingestion</h3>
                <p>
                    <code>--check</code> loads the file through the same
                    streaming pipeline the TUI uses and prints size, node
                    count, elapsed time, and throughput. Non-TUI. Useful
                    in CI and ad-hoc perf checks.
                </p>
                <pre><code>twig --check huge.json</code></pre>
            </section>

            <section id="keybindings">
                <h2>Keyboard shortcuts</h2>
                <p>Twig is designed for keyboard-centric workflows.</p>

                <div class="table-wrapper">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Context</th>
                                <th>Action</th>
                                <th>Key</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>General</strong></td>
                                <td>Help / cheatsheet</td>
                                <td><code>?</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td></td>
                                <td>Quit</td>
                                <td><code>q</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td></td>
                                <td>Toggle theme</td>
                                <td><code>t</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Navigation</strong></td>
                                <td>Move selection</td>
                                <td><code>↑ ↓ ← →</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td></td>
                                <td>Drill in / expand</td>
                                <td><code>→</code> / <code>Enter</code> / <code>l</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td></td>
                                <td>Step back / collapse</td>
                                <td><code>←</code> / <code>Esc</code> / <code>h</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td></td>
                                <td>Jump to top / bottom</td>
                                <td><code>g</code> / <code>G</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td></td>
                                <td>Smart jump to path</td>
                                <td><code>:</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Search</strong></td>
                                <td>Global search</td>
                                <td><code>/</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td></td>
                                <td>Next match</td>
                                <td><code>n</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td></td>
                                <td>Previous match</td>
                                <td><code>N</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Actions</strong></td>
                                <td>Copy path</td>
                                <td><code>c</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td></td>
                                <td>Copy source slice</td>
                                <td><code>y</code></td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="cli">
                <h2>CLI reference</h2>
                <pre><code>twig [OPTIONS] &lt;FILE&gt;</code></pre>

                <h3>Arguments</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <code>&lt;FILE&gt;</code> — path to a JSON or YAML file
                    </li>
                </ul>

                <h3>Options</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <code>-p</code>, <code>--print</code> — print
                        pretty-printed output to stdout (non-TUI)
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <code>-o</code>, <code>--output &lt;FILE&gt;</code> —
                        write to <code>FILE</code> instead of stdout (with
                        <code>--print</code> or <code>--fix</code>)
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <code>-i</code>, <code>--indent &lt;N&gt;</code> —
                        indent width for pretty-printed or fixed output
                        (default: 2)
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <code>--fix</code> — attempt to repair malformed
                        JSON and write a clean copy
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <code>--check</code> — load and print size, node
                        count, elapsed, throughput (non-TUI)
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <code>--rebuild-db</code> — drop the on-disk
                        SQLite cache before loading
                    </li>
                    <li><code>--version</code> — print version and exit</li>
                    <li><code>--help</code> — print help and exit</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="configuration">
                <h2>Configuration</h2>
                <p>
                    Twig is zero-config by default. It persists your
                    settings between sessions.
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Theme</strong> — <strong
                            >Catppuccin Mocha</strong
                        > (default) and
                        <strong>Solarized Dark</strong> ship in the box.
                        Cycle with <code>t</code>. Your choice is saved
                        and restored on the next launch.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Config file</strong> — stored at
                        <code>~/.config/twig/config.json</code> (or the
                        platform equivalent). Shared with the legacy
                        Python implementation, so theme preference carries
                        over when upgrading from <code>twg</code>.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Cache</strong> — a SQLite file with an
                        FTS5 virtual table, stored under the platform's
                        cache directory. Safe to delete at any time;
                        rebuilt on the next launch.
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="troubleshooting">
                <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>

                <h3>Loader errors</h3>
                <p>
                    If Twig can't parse a file, it shows the error in-app
                    (press any key to exit) and prints a colored message
                    with a <code>--fix</code> hint to stderr with exit
                    code 1. Shell pipelines see the failure immediately
                    without having to inspect TUI screenshots.
                </p>

                <h3>"Database is locked" error</h3>
                <p>
                    If Twig crashes or is killed forcefully, the SQLite
                    lock might remain. Run with
                    <code>--rebuild-db</code> to reset.
                </p>

                <h3>Clipboard not working</h3>
                <p>
                    On Linux headless servers without a clipboard daemon,
                    the copy actions (<code>c</code>, <code>y</code>) fall
                    back to writing the payload to a temporary file and
                    printing its path. The TUI shows a hint when this
                    fallback fires.
                </p>

                <h3>Cross-compile issues when building from source</h3>
                <p>
                    The <code>Cargo.toml</code> in the repo points the
                    <code>aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu</code> linker at
                    <code>aarch64-linux-gnu-gcc</code>. To cross-compile
                    locally on Debian/Ubuntu, install the cross tools:
                </p>
                <pre><code>sudo apt-get install -y gcc-aarch64-linux-gnu libc6-dev-arm64-cross</code></pre>
                <p>
                    The CI uses <a
                        href="https://github.com/cross-rs/cross"
                        rel="noopener"><code>cargo-cross</code></a
                    >, which pulls a Docker image with the right
                    toolchain preinstalled.
                </p>
            </section>

            <footer class="docs-footer">
                <p>
                    Found a bug or have a feature request?
                    <a
                        href="https://github.com/workdone0/twig/issues"
                        rel="noopener">Open an issue on GitHub</a
                    >.
                </p>
            </footer>
        </article>
    </div>
</Layout>

<style>
    .docs {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: 1fr;
        gap: 1.5rem;
        margin-top: 1.5rem;
        margin-bottom: var(--space-section);
    }

    @media (min-width: 960px) {
        .docs {
            grid-template-columns: 220px 1fr;
            gap: 3rem;
            margin-top: 3rem;
        }
    }

    /* Mobile TOC: a collapsible details/summary block, hidden on desktop */
    .docs-toc-mobile {
        background: var(--bg-surface);
        border: 1px solid var(--border-color);
        border-radius: 10px;
        padding: 0;
        margin-bottom: 1rem;
    }

    .docs-toc-mobile summary {
        list-style: none;
        cursor: pointer;
        padding: 0.875rem 1rem;
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: space-between;
        font-family: var(--font-mono);
        font-size: 0.85rem;
        color: var(--text-primary);
    }

    .docs-toc-mobile summary::-webkit-details-marker {
        display: none;
    }

    .docs-toc-mobile[open] summary svg {
        transform: rotate(180deg);
    }

    .docs-toc-mobile summary svg {
        transition: transform 0.2s;
    }

    .docs-toc-mobile ul {
        list-style: none;
        padding: 0 1rem 0.75rem;
        margin: 0;
    }

    .docs-toc-mobile li {
        margin: 0;
        padding: 0;
        border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-color);
    }

    .docs-toc-mobile li:last-child {
        border-bottom: 0;
    }

    .docs-toc-mobile a {
        display: block;
        padding: 0.625rem 0;
        color: var(--text-secondary);
        font-size: 0.9rem;
    }

    .docs-toc-mobile a:hover {
        color: var(--accent-color);
        opacity: 1;
    }

    /* Desktop TOC: hidden on mobile */
    .docs-toc {
        display: none;
    }

    @media (min-width: 960px) {
        .docs-toc-mobile {
            display: none;
        }
        .docs-toc {
            display: block;
            position: sticky;
            top: 80px;
            align-self: start;
        }
    }

    .toc-label {
        font-family: var(--font-mono);
        font-size: 0.7rem;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        letter-spacing: 0.08em;
        color: var(--text-muted);
        margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    }

    .docs-toc ul {
        list-style: none;
        padding: 0;
        border-left: 1px solid var(--border-color);
    }

    .docs-toc li {
        margin: 0;
    }

    .docs-toc a {
        display: block;
        padding: 0.4rem 0 0.4rem 0.875rem;
        margin-left: -1px;
        border-left: 1px solid transparent;
        color: var(--text-secondary);
        font-size: 0.875rem;
        transition: all 0.15s;
    }

    .docs-toc a:hover {
        color: var(--text-primary);
        border-left-color: var(--border-focus);
        opacity: 1;
    }

    /* Article */
    .docs-content {
        max-width: 100%;
        min-width: 0;
    }

    .docs-header {
        margin-bottom: 3rem;
        padding-bottom: 1.5rem;
        border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-color);
    }

    .docs-header h1 {
        font-size: var(--fs-hero);
        font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw + 1rem, 3rem);
    }

    .eyebrow {
        display: inline-block;
        font-family: var(--font-mono);
        font-size: 0.7rem;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        letter-spacing: 0.1em;
        color: var(--accent-color);
        margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    }

    .lead {
        font-size: 1.05rem;
        color: var(--text-secondary);
        /* Inherit line-height from body (1.65) — don't override. */
        max-width: 640px;
        margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    }

    section {
        margin-bottom: 3rem;
        scroll-margin-top: 80px;
    }

    section h2 {
        font-size: 1.5rem;
        /* Zero padding-bottom + 1px border + 15px margin-bottom =
           16px text-to-text gap to the next element — same as
           paragraph-to-paragraph, so the rhythm is uniform. The
           border line sits right under the heading text. */
        margin-bottom: 0.9375rem;
        padding-bottom: 0;
        border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-color);
    }

    section h3 {
        font-size: 1rem;
        /* No margin-top: the preceding element's margin-bottom
           already provides the gap, so h3 sits at the natural
           baseline after a paragraph instead of pushing itself
           down 2rem. Eliminates the alternating 2rem/1.75rem
           rhythm between heading and paragraph pairs. */
        margin-top: 1.5rem;
        margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
        font-family: var(--font-mono);
        color: var(--text-primary);
    }

    section h3:first-child {
        /* When an h3 is the very first thing in a section, sit it
           right under the h2 border without the 1.5rem top. */
        margin-top: 1rem;
    }

    p {
        margin-bottom: 1rem;
    }

    ul,
    ol {
        margin: 1rem 0;
    }

    /* Tables */
    .table-wrapper {
        border: 1px solid var(--border-color);
        border-radius: 8px;
        overflow-x: auto;
        -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch;
        max-width: 100%;
        /* 1.5rem vertical margin, matching <pre>, so a table
           between two paragraphs has the same spacing as a code
           block between two paragraphs. */
        margin: 1.5rem 0;
    }

    table {
        width: 100%;
        border-collapse: collapse;
        font-size: 0.9rem;
        min-width: 480px;
    }

    th,
    td {
        /* 0.625rem ≈ a 1rem line / 1.6 = 0.625, so cell padding
           sits on the same baseline grid as paragraph line-height. */
        padding: 0.625rem 1rem;
        text-align: left;
        border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-color);
    }

    tr:last-child td {
        border-bottom: 0;
    }

    th {
        background: var(--bg-surface);
        color: var(--text-primary);
        font-weight: 600;
        font-size: 0.75rem;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    }

    td code {
        font-family: var(--font-mono);
        font-size: 0.85em;
        color: var(--text-primary);
    }

    /* Block code — its own rhythm unit. 1.5rem vertical margin
       so two pre blocks back-to-back and a pre → paragraph both
       read with the same spacing. */
    pre {
        margin: 1.5rem 0;
    }

    pre code {
        background: transparent;
        border: 0;
        padding: 0;
        color: var(--text-primary);
        word-break: normal;
    }

    /* Inline code: the global.css rules already give the right
       treatment (no border, no vertical padding, soft accent
       background). Don't override them with border/padding
       rules here — that was the bug. */

    /* Docs-page header rhythm */
    .docs-header h1 {
        /* Looser than the global h1 (1.15) but tighter than
           body (1.65). 1.25 keeps the multi-line header readable
           without compressing it against the lead below. */
        line-height: 1.25;
        margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    }

    .lead {
        /* Inherit line-height from body so it matches the rest of
           the content (was 1.65, now inherits 1.65 from body — no
           override needed). Set an explicit bottom margin so the
           gap between the docs header and the first section is
           consistent with section-to-section spacing. */
        margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    }

    .docs-footer {
        border-top: 1px solid var(--border-color);
        padding-top: 1.5rem;
        margin-top: 3rem;
        font-size: var(--fs-small);
        color: var(--text-muted);
    }
</style>
